python3-paramiko-insecure
=========================

Read this before using the package.

A connection made with paramiko_insecure is roughly as private and as
trustworthy as telnet or rsh. The algorithms it restores are broken or badly
weakened: an attacker who can see the traffic, or sit in the middle of it, can
be expected to read the session and to impersonate either end. The encryption
is theatre; do not rely on it.

Therefore:

  * Use it only on a physically secure or otherwise isolated network -- a lab
    bench, a management VLAN with no route off it, or a direct cable.
  * Never use it across the public internet, or over any network you would not
    be willing to send the same commands over telnet.
  * Assume every password and every byte typed into such a session is
    disclosed, and that anything it reports back may have been altered.
  * Where a device supports anything better, use python3-paramiko instead.

Why it exists
-------------

Some equipment is 20 years or older, installed where replacing it is not
possible -- remote sites, sealed enclosures, machinery whose vendor is long
gone -- and its SSH server speaks nothing newer. The choice is not between
this package and a secure connection; it is between this package and telnet,
or no access at all.

What is restored, relative to paramiko 5
----------------------------------------

  * ssh-dss (DSA) host and user keys, including DSSKey;
  * ssh-rsa (RSA with SHA-1) signatures;
  * diffie-hellman-group1-sha1, diffie-hellman-group14-sha1 and
    diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1 key exchange;
  * group-exchange moduli down to 1024 bits (paramiko 5 requires 2048).

CBC ciphers (aes*-cbc, 3des-cbc) and hmac-sha1/hmac-md5 were never removed
upstream and are available in both packages.

GSSAPI/Kerberos is NOT restored. The scope here is core SSH, SFTP and SCP.

This package uses its own copy of cryptography, python3-cryptography-insecure,
installed as "cryptography_insecure", rather than the system one. Upstream
cryptography is retiring what is needed here: DSA is deprecated as of version
49, and 3DES has already moved to hazmat.decrepit. The private copy means
python3-cryptography can follow those removals without breaking this package,
and that nothing else on the machine is held back by it.

Modern algorithms are still preferred: an obsolete one is only negotiated when
the device offers nothing better. Nothing is gained by disabling the good ones.

Using it
--------

The module is installed as "paramiko_insecure", never as "paramiko", so this
package changes nothing for existing users of python3-paramiko. Import it by
its own name, and do NOT alias it:

    import paramiko_insecure                      # yes
    import paramiko_insecure as paramiko          # NO

Aliasing hides which connections are unprotected, which is the one thing a
reader of the code most needs to see. Keep the name at every call site:

    client = paramiko_insecure.SSHClient()
    client.load_host_keys("/path/to/known_hosts")
    client.set_missing_host_key_policy(paramiko_insecure.RejectPolicy())
    client.connect("old-switch.example", username="admin", password="...")

Passwords: an SSH key would be better, but equipment this old frequently
supports password authentication only, so password login is left enabled.
Assume any password used over such a connection is compromised, and do not
reuse it anywhere else.

Host keys: still check them. Verification is weak here (a DSA or SHA-1 RSA
host key can be forged by a capable attacker), but it does detect the ordinary
case of talking to the wrong box.

RSA user keys and servers that advertise more than they accept: OpenSSH 9.2
(Debian bookworm) sends rsa-sha2-256/512 in server-sig-algs even when its
PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms only allows ssh-rsa, and paramiko believes the
advertisement. If RSA authentication is rejected by such a server, force SHA-1
signatures:

    client.connect(..., disabled_algorithms={
        "pubkeys": ["rsa-sha2-512", "rsa-sha2-256"]})

Servers too old to know rsa-sha2 do not send server-sig-algs at all, and then
ssh-rsa is chosen automatically.

Loggers are named "paramiko_insecure.*", so their output can be told apart
from the stock module's.

Source and apt repository: https://github.com/mithro/paramiko-insecure
